{"title":"2018 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 4b","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Based_on_Official_Solution)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Based on Official Solution)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_4\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2018 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=T-1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A Bauman number is a positive integer each of whose digits is 1 or 2. Each Bauman number consists of blocks of digits. Each block contains at least one digit and includes all of the consecutive equal digits. For example, 2222111112111 is a 13-digit Bauman number consisting of four blocks: a block of four 2s, then a block of five 1s, then a block of one 2, then a block of three 1s; 2222222 is a 7-digit Bauman number consisting of a single block of seven 2s.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4b&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>How many 10-digit Bauman numbers consist of fewer than three blocks?\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Based_on_Official_Solution.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Based_on_Official_Solution)\">Solution 1 (Based on Official Solution)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4b&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Based on Official Solution)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can realize that there are two cases where a Bauman number could have fewer than three blocks: one where there is only one block, and another where there are two blocks.\n</p><p>From the description, we can realize that the only way that a Bauman number can have only one block is where all digits are equal. This means that the number can either have all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s or all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possibilities.\n</p><p>Next, we look at the case where the Bauman number has two blocks. This happens either when there is one block with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s followed by another block with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s or the other way around. We can look at the first case, as we can realize these are symmetrical because the other case can just be flipped to produce the other case.\n</p><p>We can realize that the block of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> can have a length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> digits in the numbers. This means that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> two block Bauman numbers where the number starts with a block of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s. \n</p><p>Combining these, there are therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/6/956387df4ac0d5acd1bd1fc31c7ab163fb593da1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9 \\times 2 = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"85\" height=\"15\" /> possibilities for the two block Bauman numbers.\n</p><p>Thus, the total number of possibilities is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/5/e956df7e592160f1303afd5dd4abb67c2d850eed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 + 18 = \\boxed{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"105\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4b&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Instead of just splitting into cases based on the number of blocks, we can realize that there's the case where the number starts with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and another starts with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. We can also realize that these cases are symmetrical as well because the other case can just be flipped to produce the other case.\n</p><p>In the first case, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> possibilities because there can be anywhere from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> digits that are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> at the start.\n</p><p>Combining these, there are therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/4/aa41143c9d64128f94b0533d8eeb6a64f5a3371e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10 \\times 2 = \\boxed{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"105\" height=\"25\" /> possibilities.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018005332\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.014 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.026 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 99/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 538/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3480/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.853      1 2018_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4\n100.00%    5.853      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:35563:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018005332 and revision id 277968. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}